abstract = {Stroke remains a leading cause of global mortality and disability, necessitating rapid and accurate diagnosis and intervention. Deep learning has emerged as a transformative technology in stroke care, offering automated analysis of medical imaging, improved diagnostic precision, and data-driven prognostic insights. This chapter provides a comprehensive overview of deep learning applications across the stroke care continuum, from detection and classification of ischemic and hemorrhagic lesions to outcome prediction and personalized rehabilitation. We explore various imaging modalities including CT and MRI, discuss architectural approaches ranging from convolutional neural networks to transformer-based models, and explore both unimodal and multimodal learning strategies. Despite remarkable progress, significant challenges persist, including data scarcity, model bias, limited generalizability, and lack of transparency. We highlight the importance of addressing these limitations through robust validation frameworks, explainable AI techniques, and standardized data-sharing protocols. Looking forward, the integration of deep learning with emerging technologies promises to enhance stroke care accessibility and effectiveness, ultimately reducing the global burden of this devastating condition.}
}